(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-12-16"] = {"16.1":{"s":"Chemistry improves our lives, but chemical processes and products can also harm health and the environment. Green chemistry is the design of chemical products and processes that reduce or remove hazardous substances. Paul Anastas, the father of green chemistry, set out twelve principles for it. The chapter then introduces nanochemistry, the chemistry of materials at the scale of billionths of a metre."},"16.2":{"s":"Sustainable development means meeting the needs of the present without harming the ability of future generations to meet theirs. Green chemistry helps by using the twelve principles: producing little waste, using less energy and safer materials, and designing products that do not stay in the environment. In this way industry can grow while protecting people and the planet."},"16.3":{"s":"The twelve principles include preventing waste, high atom economy, less hazardous synthesis, safer chemicals, safer solvents and helpers, energy efficiency, renewable feedstocks, fewer derivatives, catalysis, design for degradation, real-time analysis to prevent pollution, and safer chemistry to prevent accidents. Atom economy tells how much of the starting material ends up in the useful product rather than in waste.","f":[{"x":"% atom economy = (formula weight of the desired product)/(sum of the formula weights of all reactants used) × 100","n":"A high value means little waste. The book's example, butan-1-ol to 1-bromobutane, is 137 u / 275 u × 100 = 49.8 %.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 16.3, p.341"}],"w":["Atom economy is not the same as percentage yield: it depends only on the balanced equation."]},"16.4":{"s":"Green chemistry helps reduce pollution at its source instead of cleaning up afterwards. It cuts the waste and the hazards of making chemicals, reduces capital expenditure, and protects the ozone layer. It also helps control global warming. Industry uses it to design safer products and cleaner processes for manufacture and for use."},"16.5":{"s":"Nanoscience is the study of matter at the atomic and molecular scale, where properties differ from those of the bulk material. The nanometre scale runs from 1 to 100 nm, and one nanometre is 10⁻⁹ m. A nanomaterial has at least one dimension in this range. Nanomaterials are zero-dimensional (nanoparticles), one-dimensional (nanowires, nanotubes) or two-dimensional (thin films). Nanochemistry joins chemistry and nanoscience.","f":[{"x":"1 nm = 10^-9 m","n":"The nanometre scale is 1 to 100 nm.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 16.5, p.345"}],"w":["Nanomaterial dimension counts the sides that are NOT confined: nanoparticles have all three sides below 100 nm."]},"16.6":{"s":"Nanoparticles show special features because their size is so small. Their colour can change: gold nanoparticles of different sizes give red, purple or other colours. A bulk sample cut into nanoparticles has a very large surface area for the same volume, so they react and catalyse faster. Their melting point falls (sodium clusters), they can be much harder than the bulk, and carbon nanotubes can act as a conductor or a semiconductor.","w":["Cutting a material into smaller pieces keeps the volume the same but greatly increases the surface area."]},"16.7":{"s":"Nanomaterials are made by two approaches. The bottom-up approach builds them atom by atom, molecule by molecule or cluster by cluster; colloidal dispersion is an example. The top-down approach breaks bulk material into finer and finer pieces until only a few atoms remain. In wet chemical synthesis, the sol-gel process makes a sol (tiny solid particles in a liquid) that sets to a gel. Microscopes such as SEM and TEM are used to study them."},"16.8":{"s":"People have made and used nanomaterials for centuries, for example the ruby-red colour of old glass and the lustre of medieval pottery. Understanding them is recent. It became possible after advanced instruments such as the electron microscope and the scanning tunnelling microscope could look at the nanoscale. The word 'nanotechnology' was first used by Norio Taniguchi in 1974."},"16.9":{"s":"Nanomaterials have many uses. They make materials stronger, lighter and cleaner (scratch-resistant lenses, crack-resistant paints) and are used in sunscreens and in electronic devices such as memory chips. Silver nanoparticles help purify drinking water by killing bacteria. The lotus leaf, whose tiny structures repel water and dirt, is the model for self-cleaning surfaces."},"16.10":{"s":"Nanotechnology has advantages: faster electronics and computing, cheaper solar power and better energy storage, and in medicine smart drugs that act faster with fewer side effects. It also has risks. Nano pollution, a kind of air pollution caused by nanotechnology, can harm living things; breathed-in nanoparticles can damage the lungs. The health effects depend on the size, chemical composition and shape of the particles."}};
